a table column gets its defaults whatever you call

-[NSTableColumn init] fell through to NSObject and left every field zero. That is not a small
column, it is a column that can never be made bigger: setWidth: clamps against _maxWidth, and a
_maxWidth of zero pins the width at zero forever, so sizeLastColumnToFit and every autoresize style
become silent no-ops. initWithIdentifier: also never called [super init], which is fixed here too.

WHO REACHES IT. wxWidgets subclasses NSTableColumn as wxNSTableColumn, and its -init calls
[super init] rather than initWithIdentifier:, read straight out of the shipping binary:

  -[wxNSTableColumn init]:
      movq  0xa54212(%rip), %rsi   ## Objc selector ref: init
      callq 0x100dc2978            ## Objc message: -[[%rdi super] init]

So EVERY wxWidgets list box and list control under this port had a zero width column.

WHAT IT LOOKED LIKE, and it is worth writing down because the appearance named the wrong defect.
Money Manager EX opens a User Interface Language dialog with correct chrome, a scrollbar and an
apparently EMPTY list. It was recorded as such, and four explanations were chased and disproved:
readdir, the main bundle URL, a missing symbol and a CFBundle stub. All four were innocent.

The list was never empty. Asking the table itself:

  before  CIDER_TABLE draw wxNSTableView clip=3x256 rows=42 frame=3x882 super=NSClipView,314x256 cols=0,
  after   CIDER_TABLE draw wxNSTableView clip=314x256 rows=42 frame=314x882 super=NSClipView,314x256 cols=311,

Forty two rows both times. The rows were three pixels wide, and the blue sliver at the top left of
the list was the selected row drawn at its true width.

THE TRACE THAT SAID SO is the other half of this patch: CIDER_TABLE draw now prints the frame, the
clip view it sits in, the autoresizing mask and every column width, because rows=42 alone had
already been printed and read as a healthy table.

VERIFIED by looking: the dialog now lists System default selected, then ar(SA) through es(ES) with
their native names, catala, cestina, dansk, Deutsch, English (United Kingdom), espanol. The Arabic,
Belarusian and Bulgarian rows show their tags and empty brackets, which is font coverage and a
separate question.
--- a/AppKit/NSTableColumn.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSTableColumn.m
@@ -63,6 +63,9 @@
 // an editor for it.. also theoretically -dealloc could've crashed as the cell
 // prototypes weren't initialized at all...
 - (instancetype) initWithIdentifier: (NSUserInterfaceItemIdentifier) identifier {
+    if ((self = [super init]) == nil)
+        return nil;
+
     _identifier = [identifier retain];
     _width = 100.0;
     _minWidth = 10.0;
@@ -74,6 +77,15 @@
     return self;
 }
 
+/* A COLUMN GETS ITS DEFAULTS WHATEVER YOU CALL, which is what macOS does. -init reached NSObject
+ * and left every field zero, and a zero maxWidth is a trap rather than a small column: setWidth:
+ * clamps against it, so nothing can ever widen the column again and sizeLastColumnToFit becomes a
+ * silent no-op. wxWidgets subclasses this and its own -init calls [super init], which is how Money
+ * Manager EX drew a list of 42 languages three pixels wide (#213). */
+- (instancetype) init {
+    return [self initWithIdentifier: @""];
+}
+
 - (void) dealloc {
     [_identifier release];
     [_headerCell release];
